# Why Use a Bus?

A bus provides a standardized communication path between different parts of a computer system.

Without a bus, every component could require its own dedicated communication interface.

For example:

Without a common bus:

CPU ─────────► RAM
CPU ─────────► UART
CPU ─────────► GPIO
CPU ─────────► Timer

With a bus:

                 CPU
                  │
                  ▼
                 BUS
          ┌───────┼───────┐
          ▼       ▼       ▼
         RAM     UART    GPIO


The bus makes it easier to connect additional components to the system.

---

# Connection to Peripherals

The same basic bus concepts can be used to communicate with peripherals.

For example:

CPU
 │
 ▼
BUS
 │
 ▼
UART

The CPU can send an address and data through the bus, and the UART can respond to that transaction.

This is the foundation for memory-mapped I/O, which will be studied in a later project.

---

# Memory-Mapped I/O

A peripheral can be assigned a specific address range.

For example:

0x00000000 → RAM
0x10000000 → UART
0x20000000 → GPIO
0x30000000 → Timer

The CPU can then communicate with a peripheral by accessing its assigned address.

For example:

CPU
 │
 │ Write 0x41
 │ Address 0x10000000
 ▼
BUS
 │
 ▼
UART

This allows peripherals to be accessed using normal memory read and write operations.

