# Reflection

This project helped me understand what actually happens when a CPU accesses memory.

In the previous project, I learned that a SoC contains a bus connecting the CPU to memory and peripherals. In this project, I went one level deeper and looked at the actual signals involved in a bus transaction.

The most important concept was understanding the difference between a read and a write.

During a write, data travels from the CPU to memory:

CPU → BUS → Memory

During a read, data travels in the opposite direction:

CPU ← BUS ← Memory

I also learned why signals such as address, read/write control and ready are needed to coordinate communication.

This made the bus architecture much less abstract and helped me understand how a CPU can interact with the rest of a computer system.

---

# Problems

One of the main challenges was understanding the timing of a bus transaction.

It was not enough to know which signals were required. I also had to understand when those signals should be valid and when the transaction should be considered complete.

Using GTKWave to inspect the signals helped me understand the relationship between the clock, address, control signals and data.

Another challenge was understanding the difference between the bus itself and the memory connected to it. The bus provides the communication path used to access the memory.

---

# What I Would Improve

I would like to make the bus more advanced in the future by supporting multiple devices and implementing a proper bus protocol.

I could also add more realistic timing and allow different components to respond at different speeds.

For now, the simplified bus provides the foundation needed to understand more advanced bus architectures.

